C = [
("h1", "13. Discipline: 1 Corinthians 5"),
("p", "Claim: Paul orders the discipline of the Corinthian church by stated aims. The church is to put the man away so that the leaven does not spread, and so that his spirit may be saved. The volume reports the phrase “deliver such an one unto Satan” as written and builds no theory beyond the text."),
("sub", "13.1 The case and the church’s fault"),
("p", "“It is reported commonly that there is fornication among you, and such fornication as is not so much as named among the Gentiles, that one should have his father’s wife” (1 Corinthians 5:1). The fault Paul first names is the church’s, and it comes before the man’s. “And ye are puffed up, and have not rather mourned, that he that hath done this deed might be taken away from among you” (1 Corinthians 5:2). The church had not acted, and Paul says it should have mourned and put the man out."),
("sub", "13.2 The judgment and the gathering"),
("p", "Paul had judged already, though absent: “For I verily, as absent in body, but present in spirit, have judged already, as though I were present, concerning him that hath so done this deed” (1 Corinthians 5:3). The act is to be done by the church, gathered: “In the name of our Lord Jesus Christ, when ye are gathered together, and my spirit, with the power of our Lord Jesus Christ” (1 Corinthians 5:4). The verse says that the church acts in the name of the Lord, together, with Paul’s spirit present. It does not name an officer who pronounces the judgment. It names the church."),
("sub", "13.3 “deliver such an one unto Satan”"),
("p", "The act is stated: “To deliver such an one unto Satan for the destruction of the flesh, that the spirit may be saved in the day of the Lord Jesus” (1 Corinthians 5:5). Two purposes are named. The first is “the destruction of the flesh.” The second, which depends on it, is “that the spirit may be saved in the day of the Lord Jesus.” Thomas Constable weighs the meanings of the first purpose. He judges that “Probably Paul meant that he had delivered the man ‘over to Satan’ for physical bodily affliction that might even result in his premature death (‘destruction’).” He also records the other view: “A third interpretation understands the word ‘body’ metaphorically as referring to the destruction of the man’s sinful nature.”"),
("p", "The volume does not choose between them. The King James Version has “flesh,” and the passage does not say whether the word means the body or the nature. What it says is that the delivering is to Satan, that its purpose is the destruction of the flesh, and that its end is that “the spirit may be saved.” The ground is the man’s salvation at the day of the Lord Jesus. The volume reports the date as Paul writes it and does not equate it with other days of the Lord in Scripture. It reports also that Paul does not say that the man’s salvation was in danger. He says only that the end of the delivering is that his spirit “may be saved.”"),
("sub", "13.4 The leaven"),
("p", "The second ground is for the church: “Your glorying is not good. Know ye not that a little leaven leaveneth the whole lump? Purge out therefore the old leaven, that ye may be a new lump, as ye are unleavened. For even Christ our passover is sacrificed for us: Therefore let us keep the feast, not with old leaven, neither with the leaven of malice and wickedness; but with the unleavened bread of sincerity and truth” (1 Corinthians 5:6–8). The language is of a feast and a lump of dough, and the figure is Paul’s. He does not say that the Body keeps the feasts of Israel. He uses the pictures to say that the church must not allow what is leavened to remain in it, and that it is to be what it is: “as ye are unleavened.”"),
("sub", "13.5 The limits of the discipline"),
("p", "Paul draws two limits. The first is that the discipline concerns “a brother.” “But now I have written unto you not to keep company, if any man that is called a brother be a fornicator, or covetous, or an idolater, or a railer, or a drunkard, or an extortioner; with such an one no not to eat” (1 Corinthians 5:11). The company that Paul says not to keep is the company of a brother who lives so. He adds that the discipline does not reach the world: “Yet not altogether with the fornicators of this world, or with the covetous, or extortioners, or with idolaters; for then must ye needs go out of the world” (1 Corinthians 5:10). The second limit is of the judge: “For what have I to do to judge them also that are without? do not ye judge them that are within? But them that are without God judgeth. Therefore put away from among yourselves that wicked person” (1 Corinthians 5:12, 13)."),
("ctbl", "1 Corinthians 5 in its parts.", [
    ("Verses", "What is said", "The ground or aim stated"),
    ("5:1, 2", "The man is to be taken away from among them", "The church is puffed up and has not mourned"),
    ("5:3, 4", "Paul has judged; the church acts gathered, in the name of the Lord", "The power of our Lord Jesus Christ"),
    ("5:5", "Deliver such an one unto Satan", "The destruction of the flesh; that the spirit may be saved in the day of the Lord Jesus"),
    ("5:6–8", "Purge out the old leaven", "A little leaven leaveneth the whole lump"),
    ("5:9–11", "Not to keep company with a brother who is such, no not to eat", "The brother, not the world"),
    ("5:12, 13", "Put away that wicked person", "The church judges those within; God judges those without"),
  ], [0.14, 0.44, 0.42]),
("sub", "13.6 What the volume takes from the chapter"),
("bul", [
    "The church is faulted before the man, and it is to act in the name of the Lord, gathered.",
    "“Deliver such an one unto Satan” is reported as written, with its two stated purposes, and no theory is built from it.",
    "The leaven is the second ground: the whole lump is at stake.",
    "The discipline concerns a brother, and the world is left to God.",
  ]),
]
